{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.6.103", "inferred_role": "compromised_workstation_marketing", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:08:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-07-05T01:23:14+0200", "agent_name": "WS-MKT-18"},
    {"ip": "104.20.67.3", "inferred_role": "external_cloudflare_cdn_fronting_pastebin_deaddrop", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:11:24+0200", "last_seen": "2028-07-05T01:23:14+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Masquerading", "technique": "T1036.005", "what_happened": "At 2028-07-05 01:08:42 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-MKT-18 (10.30.6.103): a process named 'svchost.exe' started under user CORP\\meriem.t with parentImage=explorer.exe and image=C:\\Users\\meriem.t\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost.exe. The REAL Windows svchost.exe lives at C:\\Windows\\System32\\svchost.exe and is ALWAYS launched by services.exe (NEVER explorer.exe). A 'svchost.exe' image in a user-writable path (AppData\\Roaming) with parent=explorer.exe is a textbook masquerading indicator — the attacker is using the legitimate system-binary name to hide a malicious binary in a path that does NOT require admin rights to write to.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 process-create rule on WS-MKT-18. The image-path field carries the literal C:\\Users\\meriem.t\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost.exe value; the parentImage field carries explorer.exe — both signals diagnostic of masquerading + user-launched (not service-launched) impersonation.", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:08:42+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Command and Control / Web Service", "technique": "T1102.001 (Dead Drop Resolver)", "what_happened": "At 01:11:18 CEST (~2 min 36s after the renamed svchost.exe started), Sysmon Event 22 (DNS query) fired on WS-MKT-18: queryName=pastebin.com, image=C:\\Users\\meriem.t\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost.exe. The masquerading binary is initiating outbound DNS resolution for pastebin.com — a known dead-drop service where attackers store base64-encoded commands at /raw/{paste_id} URLs that implants periodically fetch.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 22 DNS query rule with queryName=pastebin.com tied to a process image that is NOT a browser AND NOT in C:\\Program Files. Pastebin.com is a low-trust, user-content-only service — a non-browser process querying it is a strong dead-drop-C2 indicator.", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:11:18+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Command and Control / Application Layer Protocol", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1102.001", "what_happened": "At 01:11:24 CEST (6 seconds after the DNS query resolved), Sysmon Event 3 (network connect) fired on WS-MKT-18: dest=104.20.67.3 (Cloudflare CDN front for pastebin.com), port=443. The masquerading svchost.exe established the TLS connection to fetch the dead-drop content. At 01:18:12, Suricata logged the HTTPS request (TLS metadata extracted): src=10.30.6.103 → host=pastebin.com, uri=/raw/8fK92aQ, user-agent='Mozilla/5.0' (default-Mozilla string commonly used by implants to blend in), process=svchost.exe (enriched by Wazuh decoder from Sysmon Event 3 tie-back).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3 destination=104.20.67.3 + dest_port=443 + image=renamed svchost.exe. Suricata TLS extraction: host=pastebin.com + uri=/raw/8fK92aQ. The /raw/ URL pattern is the literal Pastebin paste-content-fetch URL — the implant's dead-drop fetch.", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:11:24+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command and Control / Dead Drop Recognized", "technique": "T1102.001", "what_happened": "At 01:18:18 CEST, the Wazuh decoder enrichment fired an alert: 'Known dead-drop URL pattern (pastebin.com/raw/*) accessed by a non-browser process (svchost.exe) located in a user-writable path' — the cross-correlation of the URL pattern + the masquerading binary triggers SOC visibility on the dead-drop C2 activity. This is the SOC's first chance to see the campaign with a single high-fidelity alert.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh decoder cross-correlation rule combining Suricata HTTPS host+uri with Sysmon Event 1 process image-path. The two-source correlation lifts a low-trust pastebin GET into a high-confidence dead-drop-C2 alert.", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:18:18+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Execution / Command and Scripting Interpreter", "technique": "T1059.001 + T1140 (Deobfuscate/Decode)", "what_happened": "At 01:18:42 CEST (30 seconds after the dead-drop fetch alert), Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-MKT-18: a process powershell.exe started with parentImage=C:\\Users\\meriem.t\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost.exe and commandLine='powershell.exe -Command \"IEX([System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString([System.Convert]::FromBase64String('JGI9R2V0L...')))\"'. The renamed svchost.exe parent + IEX([System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString([System.Convert]::FromBase64String(...))) child = the implant decoded the base64 paste content and pipes it to PowerShell IEX for in-memory execution. This is T1140 (Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information) chained with T1059.001 (PowerShell). The content of the paste (8fK92aQ) IS the attacker's next-stage command — fetched on-demand, never written to disk.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with parentImage=renamed svchost.exe + image=powershell.exe + commandLine containing 'IEX', 'FromBase64String', and 'UTF8.GetString'. The three substrings combined are the canonical PowerShell base64-decode-then-IEX pattern.", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:18:42+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Command and Control / Beacon Cadence", "technique": "T1102.001", "what_happened": "At 01:23:14 CEST, the SAME pattern repeated: Sysmon Event 22 DNS query for pastebin.com, Sysmon Event 3 connect to 104.20.67.3:443, Suricata HTTPS to host=pastebin.com uri=/raw/8fK92aQ — all from the renamed svchost.exe. The 5-minute (296-second) gap between the first fetch (01:18:12) and this second fetch (01:23:14) is consistent with a periodic implant poll interval. The same paste ID (8fK92aQ) is reused = the C2 channel is a fixed paste rather than a rotating set; the attacker pushes new content into the existing paste between polls.", "chain_reason": "Repeat-cadence correlation: same agent + same image + same destination host + same URI + ~5-minute periodicity = high-confidence beacon. Distinguishable from legitimate periodic services because (a) the process image is a renamed binary in user AppData, (b) the destination is a paste-content-only service which legitimate enterprise software does not poll, (c) periodicity is too regular for human interaction.", "first_seen": "2028-07-05T01:23:14+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2028-07-05T01:08:42+0200", "end": "2028-07-05T01:23:14+0200"},
